Derive the following Entropy Production, Irreversibility, and the Arrow of Time relation from the preceding result, showing the algebra, calculus, counting, or thermodynamic identity used at each step.
\[
\Delta S_{\mathrm{univ}}
=\Delta S_{\mathrm{system}}+\Delta S_{\mathrm{surroundings}}
=S_{\mathrm{gen,univ}}\geq0.
\tag*{[4]}
\]
Notation: \(S,\Delta S\) denotes system entropy and its finite change; unit \(\mathrm{J K^{-1}}\); \(\delta Q,Q\) denotes infinitesimal and finite heat transferred into the system; unit \(\mathrm{J, J}\); \(T_b\) denotes absolute boundary temperature at which heat crosses; unit \(\mathrm{K}\); \(S_{\mathrm{gen}},S_{\mathrm{gen,univ}}\) denotes entropy generated inside the chosen system and inside system plus surroundings; unit \(\mathrm{J K^{-1}, J K^{-1}}\); \(\Delta S_{\mathrm{isolated}}\) denotes entropy change of an isolated system; unit \(\mathrm{J K^{-1}}\); \(\Delta S_{\mathrm{system}},\Delta S_{\mathrm{surroundings}},\Delta S_{\mathrm{univ}}\) denotes entropy changes of the system, surroundings, and their composite; unit \(\mathrm{J K^{-1}, J K^{-1}, J K^{-1}}\); \(T_H,T_C\) denotes hot- and cold-body temperatures, with T_H\gt T_C; unit \(\mathrm{K, K}\).

Derive the following Entropy Production, Irreversibility, and the Arrow of Time relation from the preceding result, showing the algebra, calculus, counting, or thermodynamic identity used at each step.
\[
\Delta S_{\mathrm{univ}}
=-\frac{Q}{T_H}+\frac{Q}{T_C}
=Q\left(\frac{1}{T_C}-\frac{1}{T_H}\right)>0.
\tag*{[5]}
\]
Notation: \(S,\Delta S\) denotes system entropy and its finite change; unit \(\mathrm{J K^{-1}}\); \(\delta Q,Q\) denotes infinitesimal and finite heat transferred into the system; unit \(\mathrm{J, J}\); \(T_b\) denotes absolute boundary temperature at which heat crosses; unit \(\mathrm{K}\); \(S_{\mathrm{gen}},S_{\mathrm{gen,univ}}\) denotes entropy generated inside the chosen system and inside system plus surroundings; unit \(\mathrm{J K^{-1}, J K^{-1}}\); \(\Delta S_{\mathrm{isolated}}\) denotes entropy change of an isolated system; unit \(\mathrm{J K^{-1}}\); \(\Delta S_{\mathrm{system}},\Delta S_{\mathrm{surroundings}},\Delta S_{\mathrm{univ}}\) denotes entropy changes of the system, surroundings, and their composite; unit \(\mathrm{J K^{-1}, J K^{-1}, J K^{-1}}\); \(T_H,T_C\) denotes hot- and cold-body temperatures, with T_H\gt T_C; unit \(\mathrm{K, K}\).

