\]

For heat \(Q>0\) transferred spontaneously from a hot body to a cold body, the hot body loses entropy and the cold body gains entropy:

Applying the stated definitions and constraint gives
\[
\Delta S_{\mathrm{univ}}
=-\frac{Q}{T_H}+\frac{Q}{T_C}
=Q\left(\frac{1}{T_C}-\frac{1}{T_H}\right)>0.
\tag*{[5]}
\]

The reverse transfer would make equation \([5]\) negative and cannot be the sole spontaneous change. This asymmetry is the thermodynamic arrow of time.

Final answer:
\[
\Delta S_{\mathrm{univ}}
=-\frac{Q}{T_H}+\frac{Q}{T_C}
=Q\left(\frac{1}{T_C}-\frac{1}{T_H}\right)>0.
\tag*{[5]}
\]

